  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1218 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nThe 12Z upper-air analysis features a 596 dm upper-level ridge\ncentered over the Southeast U.S today.  This has resulted in a\ncontinuation of hot and dry conditions across the Mid-South.\nMeanwhile, Tropical Depression Edouard is located near the Upper\nTexas Gulf Coast. Noon temperatures are in the 90s across the\nentire area with surface dewpoints mainly in the upper 60s to\nlower 70s.\n\nAnother hot afternoon is expected across the area with highs in\nupper 90s to around 100 degrees. Observations over the past\n24 hours continue to highlight a high bias with NBM dewpoints\ntoday as drier air has been mixing to the surface. Thus, the\ndewpoints for the next 24 hours have been adjusted downward\naccordingly to better reflect trends. While forecast heat index\nvalues have trended lower than initially expected, they will\nstill reach advisory criteria of 105 to 106 degrees in many\nlocations.\n\nHot and mainly dry conditions are expected to persist into the\nupcoming weekend with the remnants of Edouard expected to remain\ndisplaced to our south and west. Humidity is expected to slowly\nrise during this period with heat index values ranging between\n105 to 109 degrees. Thus, the Heat Advisory is expected to remain\nin effect through at least 8 PM Saturday. Overall shear is\nexpected to remain weak through the period with confidence\nremaining low (< 20% chance) for isolated convective development\noccurring during peak heating.\n\nLong-term model trends indicate the upper-level ridge will\nretrograde west into the upcoming weekend. This will allow for a\nslight reduction in the hot temperatures by late weekend with\nrain chances remaining low at best well into next week as the\nupper-level ridge reestablishes itself over the region.",
